has killed more than 10,000 Ukrainians and displaced millions more. The con=
flict has also seen Ukraine become a <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/story=
/russian-hackers-attack-ukraine/">scorched-earth testing ground</a> for Rus=
sian cyberwar tactics. In 2015 and 2016, while the Kremlin-linked hackers k=
nown as <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/story/mueller-indictment-dnc-hack-=
russia-fancy-bear/">Fancy Bear</a> were busy breaking into the US Democrati=
c National Committee=E2=80=99s servers, another group of agents known as <a=
 href=3D"https://www.wired.com/story/russian-hacking-teams-infrastructure/"=
>Sandworm</a> was hacking into dozens of Ukrainian governmental organizatio=
ns and companies. They penetrated the networks of victims ranging from medi=
a outlets to railway firms, detonating logic bombs that destroyed terabytes=
 of data. The attacks followed a sadistic seasonal cadence. In the winters =
of both years, the saboteurs capped off their destructive sprees by causing=
 widespread power outages=E2=80=94the first confirmed blackouts induced by =
hackers.</p><p class=3D"paywall">But those attacks still weren=E2=80=99t Sa=
ndworm=E2=80=99s grand finale. In the spring of 2017, unbeknownst to anyone=
