><p class=3D"paywall">NotPetya=E2=80=99s architects combined that digital s=
keleton key with an older invention known as <a href=3D"https://www.wired.c=
om/story/how-mimikatz-became-go-to-hacker-tool/">Mimikatz</a>, created as a=
 proof of concept by French security researcher Benjamin Delpy in 2011. Del=
py had originally released Mimikatz to demonstrate that Windows left users=
=E2=80=99 passwords lingering in computers=E2=80=99 memory. Once hackers ga=
ined initial access to a computer, Mimikatz could pull those passwords out =
of RAM and use them to hack into other machines accessible with the same cr=
edentials. On networks with multiuser computers, it could even allow an aut=
omated attack to hopscotch from one machine to the next.</p><p class=3D"pay=
wall">Before NotPetya=E2=80=99s launch, Microsoft had released a patch for =
its EternalBlue vulnerability. But EternalBlue and Mimikatz together noneth=
eless made a virulent combination. =E2=80=9CYou can infect computers that a=
ren=E2=80=99t patched, and then you can grab the passwords from those compu=
ters to infect other computers that <em>are</em> patched,=E2=80=9D Delpy sa=
ys.</p><p class=3D"paywall">NotPetya took its name from its resemblance to =
the <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/story/petya-ransomware-outbreak-eterna=
l-blue/">ransomware Petya</a>, a piece of criminal code that surfaced in ea=
rly 2016 and extorted victims to pay for a key to unlock their files. But N=
otPetya=E2=80=99s ransom messages were only a ruse: The malware=E2=80=99s g=
oal was purely destructive. It irreversibly encrypted computers=E2=80=99 ma=
ster boot records, the deep-seated part of a machine that tells it where to=
 find its own operating system. Any ransom payment that victims tried to ma=
ke was futile. No key even existed to reorder the scrambled noise of their =
computer=E2=80=99s contents.</p><div class=3D"GenericCalloutWrapper-tojWn e=
fJA-DB callout--has-top-border" data-testid=3D"GenericCallout" data-event-b=
oundary=3D"click" data-event-click=3D"{&quot;pattern&quot;:&quot;GenericCal=
