de-experiments=3D"true"><div class=3D"BlockquoteEmbedContent-esRbGs dqgvoS =
blockquote-embed__content"><p>The weapon=E2=80=99s target was Ukraine. But =
its blast radius was the entire world. =E2=80=9CIt was the equivalent of us=
ing a nuclear bomb to achieve a small tactical victory,=E2=80=9D Bossert sa=
ys.</p></div></blockquote></aside></div><p class=3D"paywall">The release of=
 NotPetya was an act of <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/tag/cyberwar/">cyb=
erwar</a> by almost any definition=E2=80=94one that was likely more explosi=
ve than even its creators intended. Within hours of its first appearance, t=
he worm raced beyond Ukraine and out to countless machines around the world=
, from hospitals in Pennsylvania to a chocolate factory in Tasmania. It =C2=
=ADcrippled multinational companies including Maersk, pharmaceutical giant =
Merck, FedEx=E2=80=99s European subsidiary TNT Express, French construction=
 company Saint-Gobain, food producer Mondel=C4=93z, and manufacturer Reckit=
t Benckiser. In each case, it inflicted nine-figure costs. It even spread b=
ack to Russia, striking the state oil company Rosneft.</p><p class=3D"paywa=
ll">The result was more than $10 billion in total damages, according to a W=
hite House assessment confirmed to WIRED by former Homeland Security advise=
r <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/2017/02/tom-bossert-trump-cybersecurity/=
">Tom Bossert</a>, who at the time of the attack was President Trump=E2=80=
=99s most senior cybersecurity-=C2=ADfocused official. Bossert and US intel=
ligence agencies also <a href=3D"https://www.wired.com/story/white-house-ru=
ssia-notpetya-attribution/">confirmed in February</a> that Russia=E2=80=99s=
 military=E2=80=94the prime suspect in any cyberwar attack targeting Ukrain=
e=E2=80=94was responsible for launching the malicious code. (The Russian fo=
reign ministry declined to answer repeated requests for comment.)</p><p cla=
ss=3D"paywall">To get a sense of the scale of NotPetya=E2=80=99s damage, co=
nsider the nightmarish but more typical ransomware attack that paralyzed th=
e city government of Atlanta this past March: It cost up to $10 million, a =
tenth of a percent of NotPetya=E2=80=99s price. Even <a href=3D"https://www=
.wired.com/tag/wannacry/">WannaCry</a>, the more notorious worm that spread=
 a month before NotPetya in May 2017, is estimated to have cost between $4 =
billion and $8 billion. Nothing since has come close. =E2=80=9CWhile there =
was no loss of life, it was the equivalent of using a nuclear bomb to achie=
ve a small tactical victory,=E2=80=9D Bossert says. =E2=80=9CThat=E2=80=99s=
 a degree of recklessness we can=E2=80=99t tolerate on the world stage.=E2=
=80=9D</p><p class=3D"paywall">In the year since NotPetya shook the world, =
WIRED has delved into the experience of one corporate goliath brought to it=
s knees by Russia=E2=80=99s worm: Maersk, whose malware fiasco uniquely dem=
onstrates the danger that cyberwar now poses to the infrastructure of the m=
odern world. The executives of the shipping behemoth, like every other non-=
Ukrainian victim WIRED approached to speak about NotPetya, declined to comm=
ent in any official capacity for this story. WIRED=E2=80=99s account is ins=
tead assembled from current and former Maersk sources, many of whom chose t=
o remain anonymous.</p></div></div></div><div class=3D"GridItem-buujkM fVLM=
