ok=3D"client-content" data-testid=3D"BodyWrapper"><div class=3D"body__inner=
-container"><p class=3D"paywall">But the story of NotPetya isn=E2=80=99t tr=
uly about Maersk, or even about Ukraine. It=E2=80=99s the story of a nation=
-state=E2=80=99s weapon of war released in a medium where national borders =
have no meaning, and where collateral damage travels via a cruel and unexpe=
cted logic: Where an attack aimed at Ukraine strikes Maersk, and an attack =
on Maersk strikes everywhere at once.</p><p class=3D"paywall"><span class=
=3D"lead-in-text-callout">Oleksii Yasinsky expected</span> a calm Tuesday a=
t the office. It was the day before Ukraine=E2=80=99s Constitution Day, a n=
ational holiday, and most of his coworkers were either planning their vacat=
ions or already taking them. But not Yasinsky. For the past year he=E2=80=
=99d been the head of the cyber lab at Information Systems Security Partner=
s, a company that was quickly becoming the go-to firm for victims of Ukrain=
e=E2=80=99s cyberwar. That job description didn=E2=80=99t lend itself to do=
wntime. Since the first blows of Russia=E2=80=99s cyberattacks hit in late =
2015, in fact, he=E2=80=99d allowed himself a grand total of one week off.<=
/p><p class=3D"paywall">So Yasinsky was unperturbed when he received a call=
 that morning from ISSP=E2=80=99s director telling him that Oschadbank, the=
 second-largest bank in Ukraine, was under attack. The bank had told ISSP t=
hat it was facing a ransomware infection, an increasingly common crisis for=
 companies around the world targeted by profit-focused cybercriminals. But =
when Yasinsky walked into Oschadbank=E2=80=99s IT department at its central=
 Kiev office half an hour later, he could tell this was something new. =E2=
=80=9CThe staff were lost, confused, in a state of shock,=E2=80=9D Yasinsky=
 says. Around 90 percent of the bank=E2=80=99s thousands of computers were =
locked, showing NotPetya=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Crepairing disk=E2=80=9D message=
s and ransom screens.</p><p class=3D"paywall">After a quick examination of =
the bank=E2=80=99s surviving logs, Yasinsky could see that the attack was a=
n automated worm that had somehow obtained an administrator=E2=80=99s crede=
ntials. That had allowed it to rampage through the bank=E2=80=99s network l=
ike a prison inmate who has stolen the warden=E2=80=99s keys.</p><p class=
=3D"paywall">As he analyzed the bank=E2=80=99s breach back in ISSP=E2=80=99=
s office, Yasinsky started receiving calls and messages from people around =
Ukraine, telling him of similar instances in other companies and government=
 agencies. One told him that another victim had attempted to pay the ransom=
. As Yasinsky suspected, the payment had no effect. This was no ordinary ra=
nsomware. =E2=80=9CThere was no silver bullet for this, no antidote,=E2=80=
=9D he says.</p><div class=3D"GenericCalloutWrapper-tojWn efJA-DB callout--=
