ading-h3">$300,000,000</div><p class=3D"paywall">Danish shipping company Ma=
ersk</p><div role=3D"heading" aria-level=3D"3" class=3D"paywall heading-h3"=
>$188,000,000</div><p class=3D"paywall">Snack company Mondel=C4=93z (parent=
 company of Nabisco and Cadbury)</p><div role=3D"heading" aria-level=3D"3" =
class=3D"paywall heading-h3">$129,000,000</div><p class=3D"paywall">British=
 manufacturer Reckitt Benckiser (owner of Lysol and Durex condoms)</p><hr c=
lass=3D"paywall"><div role=3D"heading" aria-level=3D"3" class=3D"paywall he=
ading-h3">$10 billion</div><p class=3D"paywall">Total damages from NotPetya=
, as estimated by the White House</p></aside></div><p class=3D"paywall">A t=
housand miles to the south, ISSP CEO Roman Sologub was attempting to take a=
 Constitution Day vacation on the southern coast of Turkey, preparing to he=
ad to the beach with his family. His phone, too, began to explode with call=
s from ISSP clients who were either watching NotPetya tear across their net=
works or reading news of the attack and frantically seeking advice.</p><p c=
lass=3D"paywall">Sologub retreated to his hotel, where he=E2=80=99d spend t=
he rest of the day fielding more than 50 calls from customers reporting, on=
e after another after another, that their networks had been infected. ISSP=
=E2=80=99s security operations center, which monitored the networks of clie=
nts in real time, warned Sologub that NotPetya was saturating victims=E2=80=
=99 systems with terrifying speed: It took 45 seconds to bring down the net=
work of a large Ukrainian bank. A portion of one major Ukrainian transit hu=
b, where ISSP had installed its equipment as a demonstration, was fully inf=
ected in 16 seconds. Ukrenergo, the energy company whose network ISSP had b=
een helping to rebuild after the 2016 blackout cyberattack, had also been s=
truck yet again. =E2=80=9CDo you remember we were about to implement new se=
curity controls?=E2=80=9D Sologub recalls a frustrated Ukrenergo IT directo=
r asking him on the phone. =E2=80=9CWell, too late.=E2=80=9D</p><p class=3D=
"paywall">By noon, ISSP=E2=80=99s founder, a serial entrepreneur named Oleh=
 Derevianko, had sidelined his vacation too. Derevianko was driving north t=
o meet his family at his village house for the holiday when the NotPetya ca=
lls began. Soon he had pulled off the highway and was working from a roadsi=
de restaurant. By the early afternoon, he was warning every executive who c=
alled to unplug their networks without hesitation, even if it meant shuttin=
g down their entire company. In many cases, they=E2=80=99d already waited t=
oo long. =E2=80=9CBy the time you reached them, the infrastructure was alre=
ady lost,=E2=80=9D Derevianko says.</p><p class=3D"paywall">On a national s=
cale, NotPetya was eating Ukraine=E2=80=99s computers alive. It would hit a=
t least four hospitals in Kiev alone, six power companies, two airports, mo=
re than 22 Ukrainian banks, ATMs and card payment systems in retailers and =
transport, and practically every federal agency. =E2=80=9CThe government wa=
s dead,=E2=80=9D summarizes Ukrainian minister of infrastructure Volodymyr =
Omelyan. According to ISSP, at least 300 companies were hit, and one senior=
 Ukrainian government official estimated that 10 percent of all computers i=
n the country were wiped. The attack even shut down the computers used by s=
cientists at the Chernobyl cleanup site, 60 miles north of Kiev. =E2=80=9CI=
t was a massive bombing of all our systems,=E2=80=9D Omelyan says.</p></div=
