">When Derevianko emerged from the restaurant in the early evening, he stop=
ped to refuel his car and found that the gas station=E2=80=99s credit card =
payment system had been taken out by NotPetya too. With no cash in his pock=
ets, he eyed his gas gauge, wondering if he had enough fuel to reach his vi=
llage. Across the country, Ukrainians were asking themselves similar questi=
ons: whether they had enough money for groceries and gas to last through th=
e blitz, whether they would receive their paychecks and pensions, whether t=
heir prescriptions would be filled. By that night, as the outside world was=
 still debating whether NotPetya was criminal ransom=C2=ADware or a weapon =
of state-sponsored cyberwar, ISSP=E2=80=99s staff had already started refer=
ring to it as a new kind of phenomenon: a =E2=80=9Cmassive, coordinated cyb=
er invasion.=E2=80=9D</p><p class=3D"paywall">Amid that epidemic, one singl=
e infection would become particularly fateful for Maersk: In an office in O=
dessa, a port city on Ukraine=E2=80=99s Black Sea coast, a finance executiv=
e for Maersk=E2=80=99s Ukraine operation had asked IT administrators to ins=
tall the accounting software M.E.Doc on a single computer. That gave NotPet=
ya the only foothold it needed.</p><p class=3D"paywall"><span class=3D"lead=
-in-text-callout">The shipping terminal</span> in Elizabeth, New Jersey=E2=
=80=94one of the 76 that make up the port-operations division of Maersk kno=
wn as APM Terminals=E2=80=94sprawls out into Newark Bay on a man-made penin=
sula covering a full square mile. Tens of thousands of stacked, perfectly m=
odular shipping containers cover its vast asphalt landscape, and 200-foot-h=
igh blue cranes loom over the bay. From the top floors of lower Manhattan=
=E2=80=99s skyscrapers, five miles away, they look like brachiosaurs gather=
ed at a Jurassic-era watering hole.</p><p class=3D"paywall">On a good day, =
about 3,000 trucks arrive at the terminal, each assigned to pick up or drop=
 off tens of thousands of pounds of everything from diapers to avocados to =
tractor parts. They start that process, much like airline passengers, by ch=
ecking in at the terminal=E2=80=99s gate, where scanners automatically read=
 their container=E2=80=99s barcodes and a Maersk gate clerk talks to the tr=
uck driver via a speaker system. The driver receives a printed pass that te=
lls them where to park so that a massive yard crane can haul their containe=
r from the truck=E2=80=99s chassis to a stack in the cargo yard, where it=
=E2=80=99s loaded onto a container ship and floated across an ocean=E2=80=
=94or that entire process in reverse order.</p><p class=3D"paywall">On the =
morning of June 27, Pablo Fern=C3=A1ndez was expecting dozens of trucks=E2=
=80=99 worth of cargo to be shipped out from Elizabeth to a port in the Mid=
dle East. Fern=C3=A1ndez is a so-called freight forwarder=E2=80=94a middlem=
an whom cargo owners pay to make sure their property arrives safely at a de=
stination halfway around the world. (Fern=C3=A1ndez is not his real name.)<=
/p><p class=3D"paywall">At around 9 am New Jersey time, Fern=C3=A1ndez=E2=
=80=99s phone started buzzing with a succession of screaming calls from ang=
ry cargo owners. All of them had just heard from truck drivers that their v=
ehicles were stuck outside Maersk=E2=80=99s Elizabeth terminal. =E2=80=9CPe=
ople were jumping up and down,=E2=80=9D Fern=C3=A1ndez says. =E2=80=9CThey =
couldn=E2=80=99t get their containers in and out of the gate.=E2=80=9D</p><=
