entral Maidenhead. When he arrived, he found that the fourth and fifth floo=
rs of the building had been converted into a 24/7 emergency operations cent=
er. Its singular purpose: to rebuild Maersk=E2=80=99s global network in the=
 wake of its NotPetya meltdown.</p><p class=3D"paywall">Some Maersk staffer=
s, Jensen learned, had been in the recovery center since Tuesday, when NotP=
etya first struck. Some had been sleeping in the office, under their desks =
or in corners of conference rooms. Others seemed to be arriving every minut=
e from other parts of the world, luggage in hand. Maersk had booked practic=
ally every hotel room within tens of miles, every bed-and-breakfast, every =
spare room above a pub. Staffers were subsisting on snacks that someone had=
 piled up in the office kitchen after a trip to a nearby Sainsbury=E2=80=99=
s grocery store.</p><p class=3D"paywall">The Maidenhead recovery center was=
 being managed by the consultancy Deloitte. Maersk had essentially given th=
e UK firm a blank check to make its NotPetya problem go away, and at any gi=
ven time as many as 200 Deloitte staffers were stationed in the Maidenhead =
office, alongside up to 400 Maersk personnel. All computer equipment used b=
y Maersk from before NotPetya=E2=80=99s outbreak had been confiscated, for =
fear that it might infect new systems, and signs were posted threatening di=
sciplinary action against anyone who used it. Instead, staffers had gone in=
to every available electronics store in Maidenhead and bought up piles of n=
ew laptops and prepaid Wi-Fi hot spots. Jensen, like hundreds of other Maer=
sk IT staffers, was given one of those fresh laptops and told to do his job=
. =E2=80=9CIt was very much just =E2=80=98Find your corner, get to work, do=
 whatever needs to be done,=E2=80=99=E2=80=89=E2=80=9D he says.</p><p class=
=3D"paywall">Early in the operation, the IT staffers rebuilding Maersk=E2=
=80=99s network came to a sickening realization. They had located backups o=
f almost all of Maersk=E2=80=99s individual servers, dating from between th=
ree and seven days prior to NotPetya=E2=80=99s onset. But no one could find=
 a backup for one crucial layer of the company=E2=80=99s network: its domai=
n controllers, the servers that function as a detailed map of Maersk=E2=80=
=99s network and set the basic rules that determine which users are allowed=
 access to which systems.</p><p class=3D"paywall">Maersk=E2=80=99s 150 or s=
o domain controllers were programmed to sync their data with one another, s=
o that, in theory, any of them could function as a backup for all the other=
s. But that decentralized backup strategy hadn=E2=80=99t accounted for one =
scenario: where every domain controller is wiped simultaneously. =E2=80=9CI=
f we can=E2=80=99t recover our domain controllers,=E2=80=9D a Maersk IT sta=
ffer remembers thinking, =E2=80=9Cwe can=E2=80=99t recover anything.=E2=80=
=9D</p><div class=3D"GenericCalloutWrapper-tojWn efJA-DB callout--has-top-b=
