_inner-container"><p class=3D"paywall">Snabe, however, didn=E2=80=99t say m=
uch about the company=E2=80=99s security posture pre-NotPetya. Maersk secur=
ity staffers tell WIRED that some of the corporation=E2=80=99s servers were=
, up until the attack, still running Windows 2000=E2=80=94an operating syst=
em so old Microsoft no longer supported it. In 2016, one group of IT execut=
ives had pushed for a preemptive security redesign of Maersk=E2=80=99s enti=
re global network. They called attention to Maersk=E2=80=99s less-than-perf=
ect software patching, outdated operating systems, and above all insufficie=
nt network segmentation. That last vulnerability in particular, they warned=
, could allow malware with access to one part of the network to spread wild=
ly beyond its initial foothold, exactly as NotPetya would the next year.</p=
><p class=3D"paywall">The security revamp was green-lit and budgeted. But i=
ts success was never made a so-called key performance indicator for Maersk=
=E2=80=99s most senior IT overseers, so implementing it wouldn=E2=80=99t co=
ntribute to their bonuses. They never carried the security makeover forward=
.</p><p class=3D"paywall">Few firms have paid more dearly for dragging thei=
r feet on security. In his Davos talk, Snabe claimed that the company suffe=
red only a 20 percent reduction in total shipping volume during its NotPety=
a outage, thanks to its quick efforts and manual workarounds. But aside fro=
m the company=E2=80=99s lost business and downtime, as well as the cost of =
rebuilding an entire network, Maersk also reimbursed many of its customers =
for the expense of rerouting or storing their marooned cargo. One Maersk cu=
stomer described receiving a seven-figure check from the company to cover t=
he cost of sending his cargo via last-minute chartered jet. =E2=80=9CThey p=
aid me a cool million with no more than a two-minute discussion,=E2=80=9D h=
e says.</p><div class=3D"GenericCalloutWrapper-tojWn efJA-DB callout--has-t=
op-border" data-testid=3D"GenericCallout" data-event-boundary=3D"click" dat=
